# Copy to apps/server/.env. Every variable below is parsed and validated by
# src/config/env.ts at startup — a missing or malformed value stops the process
# before it listens, with the offending field named.
#
# IN THE DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENTS THIS FILE DOES NOT EXIST. Both API stacks run on
# one EC2 box (file 38); every secret lives in SSM Parameter Store as a
# SecureString under /kidlearn/prod/ or /kidlearn/dev/, and each deploy writes
# its own environment's parameters into /opt/kidlearn/<env>/app.env (root-owned,
# mode 0600) for Compose to read as an env_file. The non-secret values are set
# inline in deploy/app/compose.yml. Nothing below is committed with a real value,
# baked into an image, or held by GitHub. The full per-environment matrix is in
# document/runbook.md.
#
# BOTH DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENTS RUN WITH NODE_ENV=production. "Development" there
# names the environment, not the Node mode — a dev deployment that sets
# non-Secure cookies is not testing what production will do. What distinguishes
# them is hostnames, database, credentials and ENABLE_API_DOCS.

# Runtime mode. One of: development | test | production.
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on.
PORT=4000

# Runtime database connection (pooled). The Prisma client imported from @kidlearn/db
# reads this at startup. Use the SAME pooled string as packages/db/.env DATABASE_URL
# (Supabase pooler, port 6543, ?pgbouncer=true). Duplicated here intentionally because
# the server runs as a separate process and needs the var in its own environment.
#
# Deployed (file 38 req 2):
#   production   Supabase ap-south-1, pooled :6543, ?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
#   development  postgresql://kidlearn:<pw>@dev-postgres:5432/kidlearn — a container
#                on the box, carrying NEITHER flag. Those two exist for Supabase's
#                PgBouncer; against a plain Postgres, pgbouncer=true needlessly
#                disables prepared statements and connection_limit=1 serialises the
#                whole app. Copying production's URL shape here is the obvious
#                mistake, and it costs an afternoon.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.pidlfhlaxtkpapulsgtj:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-1-ap-southeast-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"

# The single browser origin allowed to call this API with credentials. Exactly
# one origin — no wildcards. Point it at the deployed apps/web URL: in production
# https://kidlearn.net, in the dev environment https://dev.kidlearn.net.
#
# The frontend is on Vercel and this API on EC2, so the two are different origins
# that share one registrable domain. Same site, different origin — which is what
# lets the session cookie keep SameSite=Lax instead of needing SameSite=None, and
# why Safari's cross-site tracking prevention never gets a say in whether a parent
# stays signed in (file 38).
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# pino log level. One of: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- better-auth / Google OAuth (file 09) ------------------------------------
# Public base URL of THIS server. better-auth derives its OAuth callback from it,
# so it must match the redirect URI registered in Google Cloud exactly.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Session/OAuth-state signing key. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Minimum 32 characters. Rotating it signs every live parent out.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Google OAuth client credentials. One-time console setup:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → create project `kidlearn-dev`.
#   2. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen → External. App name "KidLearn",
#      add your own email as support contact. Scopes: openid, email, profile.
#      Add yourself under "Test users" while the app is unpublished.
#   3. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application.
#   4. Authorized JavaScript origins:  http://localhost:3000
#      Authorized redirect URIs:       http://localhost:4000/api/auth/callback/google
#   5. Copy the client ID and secret into the two variables below.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Path on WEB_ORIGIN the browser lands on after a successful sign-in.
PARENT_POST_LOGIN_PATH=/parent

# --- Rewards and streaks (files 23, 27) --------------------------------------
# The IANA timezone every "day" in the product is measured in: the once-a-day
# coin grant and the streak roll-over both read it, so they cannot disagree about
# where midnight is. One zone for the whole deployment — per-child timezones are
# post-MVP. A name the platform's ICU data does not know is rejected at boot.
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Dhaka

# --- API documentation (file 12a) --------------------------------------------
# Swagger UI at /docs and the raw spec at /docs.json are always available outside
# production. In production they are off unless this is exactly the string "true",
# because the docs describe the entire API surface. Values other than "true" or
# "false" are rejected at boot.
#
# Both deployed environments run NODE_ENV=production, so this flag is the whole
# difference: production sets "false" (api.kidlearn.net/docs must 404) and dev
# sets "true" (api.dev.kidlearn.net/docs is where you read it).
ENABLE_API_DOCS=false

# --- Scheduled jobs (file 30) ------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret guarding POST /api/admin/jobs/weekly-reports. Required — the
# server will not boot without it, deliberately: an optional secret would leave
# the job endpoint open whenever a deployment forgot the variable.
#
# Deliberately NOT an admin session: the caller is an external scheduler with no
# browser.
#
# The schedule is a cron entry ON THE EC2 BOX, not an external service (file 38
# req 17) — one fewer account to hold a credential, and the secret never leaves
# SSM. Production only; dev runs no scheduled jobs, so trigger it by hand there.
# Setup, once, per document/runbook.md:
#   1. openssl rand -base64 32   → write it to /kidlearn/prod/CRON_SECRET in SSM
#      Parameter Store as a SecureString. A DIFFERENT value for /kidlearn/dev/.
#   2. Add the root crontab entry on the box:
#      0 2 * * 1  curl -fsS -X POST \
#        -H "Authorization: Bearer $(...read the SSM parameter...)" \
#        https://api.kidlearn.net/api/admin/jobs/weekly-reports
#      Mondays 02:00 — the box's clock is UTC, so the entry is written in
#      Asia/Dhaka via CRON_TZ. The runbook carries the exact line.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Admin seeding (file 31) --------------------------------------------------
# Read by `pnpm --filter server seed:admin` only — NOT by src/config/env.ts, and the
# server boots fine without them. Deliberately outside the boot-time schema: a
# deployment must not need an admin password present just to start, and a stale
# password sitting in the server's own environment is a credential nobody rotates.
#
# Admins never self-register — better-auth's password sign-up is disabled for
# every caller (see emailAndPassword in src/config/auth.ts), so this script is the
# only way an administrator comes into existence. It is also how a forgotten
# password is recovered: re-run it with a new ADMIN_PASSWORD and the credential
# hash is replaced. There is no self-service reset flow.
#
# Prefer passing them inline so they never land in a file:
#   ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com ADMIN_PASSWORD='...' ADMIN_NAME='Your Name' \
#     pnpm --filter server seed:admin
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD must be at least 12 characters — the floor is enforced by both
# the script and better-auth, because these accounts still have no rate limiting
# and no second factor at all. Per-IP rate limiting on the auth routes is
# deliberately out of scope for file 38's deployment work (it is a code change
# with its own tests) and is proposed as file 41 — improvement-plan.md P1-2.
#
# THESE THREE STAY OUT OF BOTH DEPLOYMENTS. They are not in SSM and not in any
# Compose file: a deployment must not need an admin password present just to
# start. Production's first admin is created once, from your own machine, against
# the production DIRECT_URL.
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_NAME=

# --- Cloudinary media host (file 33) ----------------------------------------
# All three are required — the server will not boot without them, deliberately:
# an optional credential would leave the media library answering 500 the first
# time an admin picked a file, with a missing variable as the invisible cause.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. cloudinary.com → sign up (the free tier is what §9 budgets for).
#   2. Dashboard → Product Environment Credentials. Copy the cloud name, API key
#      and API secret below.
#   3. Settings → Upload → keep "Unsigned uploading" OFF. Every upload this app
#      performs is signed by POST /api/admin/media/sign, so an unsigned preset
#      would be a second, unauthenticated door into the account.
#
# THE SECRET NEVER LEAVES THE SERVER. It signs the upload parameters the admin's
# browser then posts straight to Cloudinary, so no file byte passes through this
# process (FR-CMS-02).
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# --- Google AI Studio: text + illustrations (files 34–36, 37a) --------------
# One key behind every generator — the text models that write lessons, stories
# and quizzes (FR-AI-01..03) and the image model that draws illustrations
# (FR-AI-05). Required: the server will not boot without it, for the same reason
# the Cloudinary credentials are required — an AI Queue that answers 500 on its
# first use is worse than a process that refuses to start and names the variable.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. aistudio.google.com → Get API key. NO billing account is needed for this
#      one, which is the point: the free tier is what §9 budgets for.
#   2. Paste it below. It is read only by apps/server — no key reaches the
#      browser, because every generation runs server-side.
#   3. aistudio.google.com/rate-limit → read your own free-tier daily request
#      limits before raising the caps at the bottom of this file. Google no
#      longer publishes them per model in the docs.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Which models the generators call. Both optional. Override to pin a snapshot or
# try a different tier without a code change — they share the key above, so they
# also share its rate limits.
GEMINI_TEXT_MODEL=gemini-3.6-flash
GEMINI_IMAGE_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-image

# --- Google Cloud Text-to-Speech (files 36, 37a — FR-AI-04, FR-I18N-05) -----
# The narration voice. The key is required; both voice names are optional and
# default to the Standard voices below.
#
# Setup, once. Unlike the AI Studio key above, THIS ONE NEEDS A BILLING ACCOUNT
# ATTACHED to the Cloud project — usage inside the free tier is still billed $0
# (4,000,000 characters a month on Standard voices, checked 2026-09-04), but the
# console will not issue the key otherwise:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → reuse the kidlearn project or create one.
#   2. Billing → link a billing account to it.
#   3. APIs & Services → Enable APIs → "Cloud Text-to-Speech API" → Enable.
#   4. Credentials → Create credentials → API key. Then "Edit API key" →
#      API restrictions → Restrict key → Cloud Text-to-Speech API only.
#   5. Paste it below. A plain string, deliberately: no service-account JSON file
#      to mount, matching every other credential in this file.
#
# If attaching billing anywhere is a hard no, file 37a's appendix documents the
# card-free fallback (self-hosted Piper) and what it costs in Bangla voice
# quality. Do not silently ship a worse voice instead.
GOOGLE_TTS_API_KEY=

# STANDARD voices, not WaveNet or Neural2: the Standard free allowance is an
# order of magnitude larger, and "child-friendly" means warm and unhurried
# rather than expensive. The language code sent to the API is derived from the
# voice name's own prefix, so the name is the whole of the setting — and a name
# from the wrong language is rejected at boot rather than accepted.
#
# PICKING THE VOICES IS AN ADMIN JUDGEMENT CALL, and the wrong choice is audible
# rather than an error anybody notices:
#   * Browse the options with:
#       gcloud text-to-speech voices list --language-code=en-US
#       gcloud text-to-speech voices list --language-code=bn-IN
#   * Audition each candidate on a real narration line, not on a sample sentence
#     — a voice that sounds pleasant reading marketing copy can be far too fast
#     for a four-year-old following words on a page.
#   * The Bangla voice must be a bn-* voice. An English voice will read Bangla
#     text, and the result plays, uploads and passes a review that was not
#     listening in that language.
#   * Keep both voices stable. A story narrated half in one voice and half in
#     another is worse than one narrated in a voice nobody loves.
GOOGLE_TTS_VOICE_EN=en-US-Standard-C
GOOGLE_TTS_VOICE_BN=bn-IN-Standard-A

# --- Daily generation caps (files 36, 37a) ----------------------------------
# All optional, with the defaults below. They exist because the batch endpoints
# turn one click into many provider calls — "generate narration" on a story is
# two clips per page — so a misclick on a long story is a day's quota. Counted
# across the whole deployment for the calendar day in APP_TIMEZONE; exceeding one
# is a 429 RATE_LIMITED, and the counter resets at local midnight.
#
# Three buckets rather than one total: the three draw on different allowances,
# and a shared ceiling would let a morning of audio work block the afternoon's
# lesson writing.
#
# THE DEFAULTS ARE DELIBERATELY LOW, and they are meant to be raised once you
# have read your own numbers. Text and image share one AI Studio account, whose
# free-tier daily request limits Google no longer publishes per model — see
# aistudio.google.com/rate-limit. The reported figure for Flash is as low as 20
# requests/day (checked 2026-09-04), and one text job may spend two requests
# (the schema-failure retry), so 8 text jobs is the ceiling that trips before
# Google's does. A 429 naming this cap is a far better admin experience than an
# opaque provider quota error surfacing as a failed generation.
#
# Audio is on a much larger and still-published allowance — 4,000,000 characters
# a month for Standard voices — so its cap is sized by what a month of daily
# batches would spend against that rather than by a request count.
#
# THE DEV DEPLOYMENT GETS LOWER CAPS THAN PRODUCTION (file 38 req 15), because
# the two share one Google Cloud TTS key — that key needs a billing account
# attached, so a second one is friction for no isolation gain. An afternoon of
# dev narration must not be able to leave an admin unable to work in production.
# Gemini and Cloudinary are NOT shared: dev has its own free AI Studio key and
# its own free Cloudinary cloud, which also keeps test uploads out of the
# production media library.
#
#                        production   development
#   AI_TEXT_JOBS_PER_DAY          8             4
#   AI_AUDIO_JOBS_PER_DAY       100            20
#   AI_IMAGE_JOBS_PER_DAY        15             5
AI_TEXT_JOBS_PER_DAY=3
AI_AUDIO_JOBS_PER_DAY=100
AI_IMAGE_JOBS_PER_DAY=15
